                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the ecosystem`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `You hold Aurelia's GEM-style dashboard: TEA <strong>14%</strong>, high-growth aspiration <strong>8%</strong>, 3-year survival <strong>42%</strong>, formalisation <strong>31%</strong>, youth unemployment 19%. The decisive fact is that activity is <strong>bimodal</strong> — roughly <em>62% necessity-driven survival microbusiness</em> and only <em>8% high-growth</em>. Drawing on Amorós's GEM research, separate the two modes and name the constraint that actually <strong>binds</strong> for each. A diagnosis that treats entrepreneurship as one thing will misfire for both — everything you decide next rests on getting this right.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Set the targeting strategy`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Choose the strategic posture. Concentrate on <strong>high-growth</strong> (productivity and exports, but a "subsidies for elites" backlash), spread across <strong>necessity</strong> microbusiness (inclusion and jobs, but flat dynamism), or design a deliberate <strong>portfolio split</strong> with an explicit logic. Tie it to the Directive's <em>twin demands</em> — new-firm formation (the necessity side moves this most) AND high-growth share (only the scalable segment moves this) — and to Aurelia's fiscal and political reality. Name the KPI your posture is built to move.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate the budget & choose the reform`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Allocate <strong>AUP 1,800M</strong> across six levers and advance exactly <strong>one</strong> regulatory reform whose fiscal cost fits <em>inside</em> the envelope. Finance guarantees, accelerators, seed capital and R&D vouchers tilt high-growth; training and formalisation support tilt necessity. The reform is a first-order lever, not an afterthought: a one-day registration reform helps a street vendor formalise; it does nothing for a fintech that struggles to finance and exit. Make the allocation, the reform and the segment you chose in Round 2 <strong>tell one story</strong> — and keep the whole costed plan within AUP 1.8B with no new debt.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the plan to the cabinet`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The 30 June submission looms. Make a 4-minute defence to three voices: the <strong>Finance Minister</strong> (fiscal discipline, no new debt), a <strong>jobs-and-inclusion coalition</strong> (visible new firms now), and an <strong>international development partner</strong> (productivity and high-growth). Show how your allocation and reform realistically move <em>both</em> headline KPIs within the budget and survive the competing demands. Good policy that breaches AUP 1.8B is just a wish list — and a plan that moves only one KPI fails the Directive.`,

                    r1_diagnosis_label:     `How do you read Aurelia's entrepreneurship?`,
                    r1_constraint_label:    `Which constraint actually binds for the HIGH-GROWTH segment?`,
                    r1_depth_label:         `How deeply do you separate the two segments in the diagnosis? (%)`,
                    r1_depth_readout:       `Segment-separation depth:`,
                    r1_depth_hint:          `A diagnosis that names the binding constraint per segment is what every later decision rests on. Amorós's point: opportunity and necessity entrepreneurship are not the same problem.`,
                    diag_bimodal:           `Bimodal — a thin high-growth layer (8%) and a vast necessity base (62%); they are different economies that respond to different levers`,
