                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_bimodal:       `<strong>That's the right reading.</strong> Aurelia is two economies: 62% necessity-driven survival firms and an 8% high-growth layer. Amorós's whole point is that they respond to different levers — a registration subsidy that helps a street vendor does nothing for a scaling fintech. Now name which constraint actually binds for each.`,
                    coach_r1_onething:      `<strong>You wrote a plan to "boost entrepreneurship" as one thing.</strong> But 62% of Aurelia's startups are necessity survival firms and 8% are high-growth — they respond to completely different levers. An undifferentiated plan misfires for both segments. Which entrepreneurship did you actually mean to grow?`,
                    coach_r1_lowtotal:      `<strong>Raising the headline activity rate is not the mandate.</strong> The Directive demands new firms AND a higher high-growth share — pushing TEA without touching dynamism just adds more survival-mode firms. The mix is the point, not the count.`,
                    coach_r1_elite:         `<strong>Dismissing necessity entrepreneurship as "noise" is its own error.</strong> It's 62% of activity, it's where the jobs and the inclusion case live, and ignoring it is what triggers the political backlash in cabinet. You can prioritise high-growth without writing off the base.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You separated the two modes AND named the high-growth segment's real constraint — access to growth finance and exit. Scaling ventures don't struggle to register; they struggle to finance and exit. That is the lens every later decision should rest on.`,
                    coach_r1_wrongcon:      `<strong>Registration cost is the necessity segment's constraint, not high-growth's.</strong> A scaling fintech can register in a morning; what it lacks is later-stage finance and an exit. Diagnose the binding constraint per segment, or you'll spend on the wrong lever.`,
                    coach_r1_demand:        `<strong>"Nothing can be done" is not a diagnosis.</strong> Finance access, the regulatory regime and skills are exactly the constraints policy can move — that's why ONEI exists. Name the one that binds for the segment you mean to grow.`,
                    coach_r1_shallow:       `<strong>A shallow diagnosis that blurs the two modes undermines everything downstream.</strong> If you don't separate necessity from high-growth now, your targeting and your allocation will conflate them too — and that is Common Error number one.`,
                    coach_r2_portfolio:     `<strong>A deliberate portfolio is the strategy the dual mandate demands.</strong> Funding both segments with an explicit logic — formation from the necessity side, dynamism from the scalable side — is how you move both KPIs at once. Now get the tilt right: too far either way and you miss one target.`,
                    coach_r2_highgrowth:    `<strong>You concentrated on a few high-growth ventures.</strong> Productivity case: strong. But youth unemployment is 19%, the opposition is already calling this "public money for the capital's tech elite," and your formation-rate target — which the Directive also set — barely moved. Will this survive cabinet?`,
                    coach_r2_necessity:     `<strong>You're funding broad survival-mode microbusiness.</strong> Inclusive and popular. But high-growth share didn't move, productivity is flat, and the development partner is asking where the scalable, exporting firms are. Amorós would note you raised activity without raising dynamism. Did you grow the economy or just the count of firms?`,
                    coach_r2_even:          `<strong>Spreading the budget evenly across everything is Common Error number one.</strong> It treats Aurelia's bimodal reality as one undifferentiated target and therefore misfires for both segments — too thin to scale a high-growth firm, too unfocused to formalise the necessity base. Pick a posture and a logic.`,
                    coach_r2_tilthigh:      `<strong>Your portfolio tilts hard toward high-growth.</strong> A deliberate split is right, but at this weight the necessity side — and the formation-rate target — gets starved, and the elite-subsidy backlash creeps back in. Pull the tilt toward the centre.`,
                    coach_r2_tiltlow:       `<strong>Your portfolio tilts hard toward necessity.</strong> A split is right, but at this weight the high-growth share barely moves and you under-serve the dynamism half of the mandate. Tilt a little more toward the scalable segment.`,
                    coach_r2_kpiformation:  `<strong>A portfolio built only to move the formation count understates your own design.</strong> You're funding both segments — say so: formation from the necessity side AND high-growth share from the scalable side. The dual KPI is the whole point.`,
                    coach_r2_kpinone:       `<strong>"Just do good things and the numbers follow" is how plans drift.</strong> The Directive named two KPIs; tie your posture to them explicitly or the cabinet — and the next fiscal cycle's review — will find no target to hold you to.`,
                    coach_r3_coherent:      `<strong>The allocation matches the posture you set.</strong> Your budget tilt and your Round-2 strategy tell one story, which is exactly what a costed plan needs. Now make sure the reform serves the same segment your pesos do.`,
                    coach_r3_incoherent:    `<strong>Your allocation contradicts your Round-2 posture.</strong> You declared one targeting strategy and then funded a different segment. A plan whose strategy and budget disagree is a plan the cabinet won't trust — align the pesos with the posture.`,
                    coach_r3_elitebacklash: `<strong>You poured almost the whole envelope into the high-growth levers.</strong> Dynamism may rise, but with the necessity base starved your formation-rate target stalls and the "subsidies for elites" story writes itself. Concentrated bets need a political answer you don't have yet.`,
                    coach_r3_survivalonly:  `<strong>Almost everything went to the necessity levers.</strong> Inclusive, but the high-growth share won't move on training and formalisation alone — those firms need finance and R&D. You're raising the count of firms without raising the economy's dynamism.`,
                    coach_r3_reformmismatch: `<strong>Your reform serves a different segment than your budget.</strong> You tilted the pesos one way and spent your one reform on the other — a registration system for a high-growth strategy, or an equity regime for a necessity strategy. Scaling startups don't struggle to register; survival firms don't need an equity exit. Match the reform to whom your budget targets.`,
                    coach_r3_overbudget:    `<strong>Your plan costs more than AUP 1.8 billion.</strong> You have 1.8 and no authority to borrow — the Finance Minister won't "find the difference." Which lever do you cut to fund the reform, and what does that do to your headline KPIs? Good policy that breaches the budget is just a wish list.`,
                    coach_r4_dualkpi:       `<strong>Framing the defence around both KPIs is the credible play.</strong> Showing formation from the necessity side and high-growth share from the scalable side — inside the budget — answers the Directive directly. Now back it with a costed line-by-line for the Finance Minister.`,
                    coach_r4_jobs:          `<strong>Leading only on jobs wins the coalition but loses the partner.</strong> The visible new-firm count is real, but the Directive also demanded high-growth dynamism — play it down and you've answered half the mandate. The development partner is still waiting for the scalable firms.`,
                    coach_r4_productivity:  `<strong>Leading only on productivity wins the partner but loses the room.</strong> Youth unemployment is 19% and the coalition wants jobs now; an exports-and-dynamism pitch that downplays formation and inclusion is the "elite subsidy" story in a different suit. Answer both demands.`,
                    coach_r4_vision:        `<strong>A bold vision with no numbers doesn't survive a Finance Minister.</strong> "An entrepreneurial Aurelia" is a slogan; the cabinet signs a costed plan that moves named KPIs inside AUP 1.8B. Bring the numbers, not the mood music.`,
                    coach_r4_costed:        `<strong>A costed, line-by-line plan inside AUP 1.8B is what the Minister can sign.</strong> No new debt, every lever and the reform accounted for — that is binding-constraint discipline. Good policy that fits the envelope beats brilliant policy that doesn't.`,
                    coach_r4_borrow:        `<strong>There is no authority to borrow.</strong> The Directive forbids new debt and the Minister won't fund a supplementary. A plan that assumes money you don't have isn't a plan — cut a lever and show what it costs your KPIs.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>"Settle the numbers later" is how the year's allocation gets forfeited.</strong> The 30 June deadline is hard; an uncosted plan misses the fiscal cycle and loses AUP 1.8B. Cost it now or lose it.`,
                    coach_r4_landed:        `<strong>That's a cabinet-ready defence.</strong> Both KPIs moved, the plan is costed inside AUP 1.8B with no new debt, and you can name the constraint each lever and the reform relieve. The Minister signs, the coalition sees the jobs, and the partner sees the scalable pipeline — the dual mandate, delivered.`,
                    coach_r4_hope:          `<strong>"More firms means more of everything" is the conflation Amorós warns against.</strong> Necessity activity does not automatically produce scalable, exporting firms — that's the whole reason the segments are different. Point to the specific high-growth levers and reform, not a hope.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>Deflecting to "the private sector's job" abandons the mandate.</strong> ONEI exists precisely to relieve the constraints that hold scalable firms back — finance, exit, talent. Name what your plan does for them, or the partner walks.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Read it as bimodal; named the high-growth segment's real constraint.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Blurred the two modes or mis-named the binding constraint.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Deliberate portfolio split tied to both KPIs.`,
                    sum_r2_concentrated:    `Concentrated on high-growth — political backlash risk.`,
                    sum_r2_necessity:       `Necessity-only spread — high-growth share stays flat.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Undifferentiated posture or no KPI logic.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Allocation coherent with posture; reform aligned; in budget.`,
                    sum_r3_mismatch:        `Reform served the wrong segment.`,
                    sum_r3_overbudget:      `Plan breached AUP 1.8B — fiscal constraint ignored.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Allocation drifted from the stated posture.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Defended both KPIs, costed, with the scalable pipeline named.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Answered only one demand or left the plan uncosted.`,

                    insight_balanced:       `Both KPIs moved and the plan held: you treated Aurelia as two economies, tilted a deliberate portfolio across them, matched the reform to the segment your budget targeted, and costed it inside AUP 1.8B with no new debt. The cabinet signs, the coalition sees the jobs, and the development partner sees a scalable pipeline — the dual mandate, delivered.`,
                    insight_undifferentiated: `You treated entrepreneurship as one undifferentiated thing — the central error. Aurelia's bimodal reality (62% necessity, 8% high-growth) means an even or unfocused plan misfires for both segments: too thin to scale a venture, too unfocused to formalise the base. Activity may flicker, but dynamism doesn't move.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `Your plan breached the AUP 1.8 billion envelope or assumed new debt the Directive forbids. However good the strategy, a plan the Finance Minister can't fund is a wish list — and a missed 30 June deadline forfeits the entire year's allocation.`,
                    insight_partial:        `You moved in the right direction but didn't fully reconcile diagnosis, targeting, allocation and reform into one story — so one of the twin KPIs lagged and the cabinet found a seam. The segments respond to different levers; the plan has to honour that end to end.`,
                    insight_elite:          `You raised the high-growth share but barely touched new-firm formation — and concentrating the budget on a few scalable ventures is exactly what triggers the "subsidies for elites" backlash. Productivity rose; the formation-rate half of the Directive, and the political room, did not.`,
                    insight_necessity:      `You lifted new-firm formation but the high-growth share stayed flat — broad survival-mode support raises activity without raising dynamism. Inclusive and popular, but Amorós's distinction cuts the other way too: the Directive demanded scalable, exporting firms, and they need finance and R&D, not just training.`,
                    insight_reformmismatch: `Your one regulatory reform served a different segment than your budget. A registration reform for a high-growth strategy, or an equity regime for a necessity strategy, wastes a first-order lever — institutional reform only pays off when it relieves the constraint the targeted segment actually faces.`,
                    insight_viability:      `Political viability ended low: the plan didn't survive the competing demands of fiscal discipline, jobs-now and high-growth dynamism. A technically sound plan that the cabinet won't sign never reaches the next fiscal cycle.`,
